A **secant** cuts the curve at two points, and its gradient is the average rate of change between them.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (4.9,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,5.5) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0.3:3.9,smooth,variable=\x] plot (\x, {0.3*\x*\x + 0.4}) node[above left] {$y=f(x)$};
\draw[thick,red] (0.6,0.172) -- (3.9,4.528) node[right,font=\small] {secant};
\fill (1.0,0.7) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (3.4,3.868) circle (2.5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A **tangent** differs by touching the same curve at just one point, and its gradient is the instantaneous rate there.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (4.9,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,5.5) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0.3:3.9,smooth,variable=\x] plot (\x, {0.3*\x*\x + 0.4}) node[above left] {$y=f(x)$};
\draw[thick,red] (1.2,0.244) -- (4.0,4.612) node[right,font=\small] {tangent};
\fill (2.6,2.428) circle (2.5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

